<h1>What I'm doing now</h1><p class="meta">Last updated: 13 November 2026 · Inspired by <a href="https://sive.rs/now" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Derek Sivers</a> and <a href="https://nownownow.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nownownow.com</a></p>
<h2>Night Owls: the 40 Day Challenge</h2><p>I joined the <a href="https://www.skool.com/nightowls" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Night Owl Nation</a> and recently completed the 40 Day Challenge. I came away with a clear brand strategy and a content plan.</p><p>The biggest lesson was sticking to one point in each story and keeping it really simple. I kept wanting to squeeze two connected ideas into one story. Choosing one made it easier to get the message across.</p><p>And a first draft is just that. It takes editing, and putting your ego aside, to cut to the quick and get to the point.</p><h2>MondayMotivator and this website</h2><p>I’ve been writing <a href="/mondaymotivator/">MondayMotivator</a> since 2004. Right now I’m bringing the archive, my books and my projects together in one place. There are a lot of stories to revisit, and plenty of ideas still to use.</p>
<h2>Your Next Move</h2><p>I’m working on <a href="/your-next-move/">Your Next Move</a>, a way to help business owners work out what to do next and follow through. I’m shaping it around what I actually do: solve problems, get ideas moving and build habits that stick. It’s still in development.</p>
<h2>The Swearing Project</h2><p><a href="/the-swearing-project/">The Swearing Project</a> is my short guide to finding The Magic Pause. It started as a personal experiment and changed how I react. I’m also working through how to turn it into a challenge people can take part in.</p>
<h2>Light Your Own Fire</h2><p>I co-wrote <a href="/light-your-own-fire/">Light Your Own Fire</a> with Charles Donoghue. I’m revisiting the book and its workbook myself, and working on making more of the material available to read here.</p>
<h2>Work</h2><p>At <a href="https://spike.co.nz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spike</a>, we help businesses communicate with their customers through email. I also work on marketing and e-commerce for <a href="https://racerproducts.co.nz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Racer Products</a> and <a href="https://scarles.co.nz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scarles</a>.</p><p>I’m using AI in the day-to-day work too. The useful bit isn’t having another tool. It’s connecting the information so I can see what needs attention and do something about it.</p>
<h2>Keeping the habits going</h2><p>These are my consecutive days completed as of 13 November. I’ve kept the routines small enough to keep doing them.</p><div class="habit-table-wrap"><table class="habit-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Habit</th><th scope="col">Consecutive days</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rebounding: 1 minute a day</td><td>375</td></tr><tr><td>Plank: 1 minute a day</td><td>1,250</td></tr><tr><td>10 Hindu push-ups a day</td><td>1,298</td></tr><tr><td>100 Hindu squats a day</td><td>1,379</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That’s 1,379 consecutive days of 100 Hindu squats. <strong>137,900 squats.</strong> Small things add up when you keep doing them.</p><p>I also do push-up and pull-up ladders three times a week, and read 10 pages a day. My tracker shows 111 and 5 respectively for those habits.</p><p>I track my habits with the <a href="https://wayoflifeapp.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Way of Life app</a>. Anyone can start with a minute a day.</p><p>And I’m still playing disc golf whenever I can. Getting a disc to do exactly what I wanted never gets old.</p> 
